<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.14.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01998</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.14.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>6c4f5226-3772-4103-ab51-6647cf270f18</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="1f35a839-0d66-476d-9347-1b9db78fa38b" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="2" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--1a-->
  <exercise data-content-id="af85d497-4717-4fc4-b6c7-2aacde1d05e9" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="The object represented by function g" data-solution-options="[&quot;The object represented by function f&quot;, &quot;The object represented by function g&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Here are graphs of functions \(f\) and \(g\).</para>
      <para>Each represents the height of an object being launched into the air as a function of time.</para>
      <para><figure><media alt="Graphs of functions f and g. Vertical axis, height. Horizontal axis, time. Function f begins low on the y axis, increases in height quickly, then ends at about halfway down the x axis. Function g begins high on the y axis, moves gradually downward, and ends toward the end of the x axis."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c2fb42c0f8f727ba961e42c44dcb9437c595eeeb"/></media></figure></para>
      <para> Which object was launched from a higher point?</para>
        </problem>
    <solution>
      <para> Correct! The object represented by function \(g\) because its \(y\)-intercepts is above the \(y\)-intercepts of the graph of \(f\).</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.3. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--1b-->
  <exercise data-content-id="4beb467c-0d2f-4c24-8881-b4da9aa1f763" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="The object represented by function f" data-solution-options="[&quot;The object represented by function f&quot;, &quot;The object represented by function g&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> Which object reached a higher point?</para>
        </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The object represented by function \(f\) because the vertex is higher.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.3. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--1c-->
  <exercise data-content-id="c35c6425-b4dc-4a7b-aad6-ad79eadee4c0" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="The object represented by function f" data-solution-options="[&quot;The object represented by function f&quot;, &quot;The object represented by function g&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> Which object was launched with the greater upward velocity?</para>
        </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The object represented by function \(f\) because the “slope” near the \(y\)-intercept is greater. It increases in height much more quickly than the other object.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.3. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--1d-->
  <exercise data-content-id="a6dc30bc-7294-4722-b69f-d4256a3b6afc" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="The object represented by function g" data-solution-options="[&quot;The object represented by function f&quot;, &quot;The object represented by function g&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> Which object landed last?</para>
        </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The object represented by function \(g\) because the positive \(x\)-intercept of its graph is to the right of the positive \(x\)-intercept of the graph of \(f\).</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.3. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--2a-->
  <exercise data-content-id="1d3584d2-ddc0-45ef-9f9f-eeacd9c57fcf" data-problem-comparator="float" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="-0.5">
    <problem>
      <para>The function \(h\) given by \(h(t)=(1−t)(8+16t)\) models the height of a ball in feet, \(t\) seconds after it was thrown.</para>
      <para>Find one of the zeros of the functions using \((8+16t)\). </para>
        <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! -0.5. From the factored form, we can tell that the graph intersects the horizontal axis at \(t=-0.5\). The zero of the function is the same value. </para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.5.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--2b-->
  <exercise data-content-id="fd7745a3-fd5a-4fb2-a162-97e44cdd044d" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="1">
    <problem>
      <para>Find one of the zeros of the functions using \((1-t)\). </para>
        <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para> Correct! 1. From the factored form, we can tell that the graph intersects the horizontal axis at \((1-t)\). The zero of the function is the same value. </para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.5.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--2c-->
  <exercise data-content-id="e1af55f5-cc64-4b14-9a0e-6834b1cdaa8b" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="The positive zero means the time, in seconds, when the basketball is on the ground or hits the ground." data-solution-options="[&quot;The positive zero means the time, in seconds, when the basketball is on the ground or hits the ground.&quot;, &quot;The negative zero represents the height, in feet, when the time is zero.&quot;, &quot;The positive zero represents the time, in seconds, when the basketball reaches the maximum height.&quot;, &quot;The negative zero represents the time, in seconds, when the basketball is on the ground or hits the ground.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> What do the zeros tell us in this situation?</para>
        </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The positive zero means the time, in seconds, when the basketball is on the ground or hits the ground.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.5.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--1d-->
  <exercise data-content-id="a699ac98-cd91-467f-9025-d5fef8e137fd" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="No" data-solution-options="[&quot;Yes&quot;, &quot;No&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> Are both zeros meaningful?</para>
        </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! No. Only the positive zero is meaningful. It tells us the ball hits the ground after 1 second. </para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.5.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--2e-->
  <exercise data-content-id="705d5199-4f67-4611-ae30-30ff51aa5a85" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="8">
    <problem>
      <para>From what height, in feet, is the ball thrown? </para>
        <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para> Correct! 8 feet. When \((1−t)(8+16t)\) is rewritten in standard form it is \(8+8t−16t^2\). The constant term (which corresponds to the \(y\)-intercept) tells us the height of the basketball when \(t=0\) or when it is launched. </para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.5. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--2f-->
  <exercise data-content-id="29628f71-310e-42f0-886f-689c67ea88a4" data-problem-comparator="float" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="0.25">
    <problem>
      <para>When, in seconds, does the ball reach its highest point? </para>
        <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para> Correct! 0.25 seconds. Graphing \(h\) shows the maximum point at (0.25,9). Or, you could recognize that 0.25 is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex because it is halfway between the two zeros, -0.5 and 1.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.5.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--2g-->
  <exercise data-content-id="5ab1f1c1-e6d5-43fa-9777-85412039fd00" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="9">
    <problem>
      <para>How high, in feet, does the ball go? </para>
        <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! 9 feet. Graphing \(h\) shows the maximum point at (0.25,9). Or, you could recognize that 0.25 is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex because it is halfway between the two zeros, -0.5 and 1. Then evaluate the function at 0.25 to get a height of 9.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.14.3 – 7.14.5.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--3a-->
  <exercise data-content-id="e3be73f2-3cb1-46b0-9b1d-e06668db6158" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="The constant term 6" data-solution-options="[&quot;The constant term 6&quot;, &quot;The linear term \\(30t\\)&quot;, &quot;The squared term \\(-16t^2\\)&quot;, &quot;The squared term \\(16t^2\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>The height in feet of a thrown football is modeled by the equation \(f(t)=6+30t−16t^2\), where time \(t\) is measured in seconds.</para>
      <para> Which term in the equation describes how many feet above the ground the football was thrown?</para>
        </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The football was thrown from 6 feet above the ground.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--3b-->
  <exercise data-content-id="4db11e62-1a36-48c7-8734-ce008601fe62" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="The linear term \(30t\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;The constant term 6&quot;, &quot;The linear term \\(30t\\)&quot;, &quot;The squared term \\(-16t^2\\)&quot;, &quot;The squared term \\(16t^2\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> Which term in the equation describes the initial upward velocity of the football in feet per second?</para>
        </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The initial upward velocity of the football was 30 feet per second. </para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--3c-->
  <exercise data-content-id="ca5820b3-7859-4636-b616-c1c22fe6dcfe" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="The squared term decreases the value of the function." data-solution-options="[&quot;The squared term decreases the value of the function.&quot;, &quot;The squared term increases the value of the function.&quot;, &quot;The squared term has no effect on the value of the function.&quot;, &quot;The value of the function is zero when \\(−16t^2\\) is zero.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> How does the squared term \(-16t^2\) affect the value of the function \(f\)? </para>
        </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The squared term decreases the value of the function because the values of \(16t^2\) are being subtracted from \(6+30t\).</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--3d-->
  <exercise data-content-id="5d14125b-5d97-4607-9fa6-d7202ce485dc" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="The influence of gravity pulling the ball down to the ground." data-solution-options="[&quot;The influence of gravity pulling the ball down to the ground.&quot;, &quot;The initial upward velocity of the football.&quot;, &quot;How far the football was thrown above the ground.&quot;, &quot;How long the ball is in the air before it hits the ground.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> What does this term \(−16t^2\) reveal about the situation?</para>
        </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The influence of gravity pulling the ball down to the ground.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--4a-->
  <exercise data-content-id="321a83e9-c3ef-4fff-a1bc-23a581947f8e" data-problem-comparator="float" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="2.25">
    <problem>
      <para>The height in feet of an arrow is modeled by the equation \(h(t)=(1+2t)(18−8t)\), where \(t\) is seconds after the arrow is shot.</para>
      <para> At what time, in seconds, does the arrow hit the ground? </para>
        <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para> Correct! The positive zero is 2.25 because \((18−8t)\) is equal to zero when \(t=2.25\). The zeros mean the times, in seconds, when the arrow is on the ground. Only the positive zero makes sense. The negative zero would represent a time before the arrow was launched.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.5.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--4b-->
  <exercise data-content-id="96622d8a-7db3-4b49-921b-b8debb4eee4a" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="18">
    <problem>
      <para>At what height, in feet, is the arrow shot? </para>
        <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! 18 feet. When the arrow is shot, \(t\) is 0 and h(0)=(1+2⋅0)(18−8⋅0)=(1)(18)=18.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.5.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--5a-->
  <exercise data-content-id="a9419388-3cfa-45be-9186-903475b2c12e" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="Object A" data-solution-options="[&quot;Object A&quot;, &quot;Object B&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>5. Two objects are launched into the air.</para>
      <para>The height, in feet, of Object A is given by the equation \(f(t)=4+32t−16t^2\).</para>
      <para>The height, in feet, of Object B is given by the equation \(g(t)=2.5+40t−16^2\).</para>
      <para>In both functions, \(t\) is seconds after launch.</para>
      <para> Which object was launched from a greater height?</para>
        </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! Object A. The objects are launched when \(t=0\). \(f(0)=4+32⋅0−16(0)2=4\) and \(g(0)=2.5+40⋅0−16(0)2=2.5\).</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.3. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--5b-->
  <exercise data-content-id="214ffa65-1827-44d9-bc43-c1b25a0f12fa" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="Object B" data-solution-options="[&quot;Object A&quot;, &quot;Object B&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> Which object was launched with a greater upward velocity?</para>
        </problem>
    <solution>
      <para> Correct! Object B.The coefficient of \(t\) gives the upward velocity and 40 is greater than 32.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.3. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Do not edit below this line-->
  <solution> </solution>
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response"> </div>
</div></section>
</content>
